 "tastes": "<p>Witbier is white beer, named for its pale, cloudy look. It comes from the farm breweries east of Brussels, around Hoegaarden, and nearly died out in the 1950s before a milkman named Pierre Celis revived it in the 1960s.</p><p>The recipe is unusual for a beer. A large share of raw, unmalted wheat and often some oats give a silky body and a haze that never settles. Dried orange peel and crushed coriander seed go in at the end of the boil. The coriander should taste citrusy and floral, not like a curry; that comes down to fresh seed and a light hand. Belgian yeast adds a peppery, slightly fruity note, and there is a gentle tartness that makes it refreshing.</p><p>Bitterness is low. The finish is dry and bubbly. It is one of the easiest craft styles to like, which is why so many Indian brewers made a wit their first canned beer.</p>",
